  "worked_solution": "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a-3$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-3}{a-3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)-(a+2)}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a-9-a-2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a-11}{a-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
